---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import QuickAnswer from "../components/QuickAnswer.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import ProductCard from "../components/ProductCard.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Self-Propelled Lawn Mowers",
    "url": "/best-self-propelled-lawn-mower/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Riding Lawn Mowers",
    "url": "/best-riding-lawn-mower/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Compact Tractors",
    "url": "/best-compact-tractor/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Gas Lawn Mowers",
    "url": "/best-gas-lawn-mowers/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Weed Eaters",
    "url": "/best-weed-eater/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-02", modified: "2026-11-02" });
const title = "6 Best Zero Turn Mowers (2026): Matched to Acreage and Budget";
const description =
  "The Toro TITAN MAX 60-inch tops 6 zero-turn mowers for 3+ acres; the Ariens IKON XD 52 and Husqvarna Z254F suit most 1 to 3 acre properties for less money.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "toro-titan-max",
    "name": "Toro TITAN MAX 60-Inch",
    "category": "Fabricated 60-in. Deck | Commercial-Grade Hydros",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Heavy fabricated steel deck resists dents and lasts for years",
      "Commercial-grade hydrostatic transaxles on a residential machine",
      "Suspension seat and wide frame for long mowing sessions",
      "60-inch width clears multiple acres quickly",
      "Broad Toro dealer network for service"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Expensive for a homeowner mower",
      "Too wide for many garden gates and some garages"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The machine to buy if you mow three acres or more every week and want it to last well over a decade.",
    "description": "The TITAN MAX is where Toro's residential zero-turns start borrowing from its commercial line. The key upgrades are a fabricated deck, welded from heavy steel plate rather than stamped from thinner sheet, and transaxles built for heavier duty cycles than the entry Toro models. Those two parts decide how long a zero-turn lasts, which is why this mower sits at the top. At 60 inches it covers a lot of ground per pass, so on three to five acres of open turf you save real time every week. The operator platform and suspension seat also matter on large properties, since you may be in the seat for well over an hour. The catch is size and price. A 60-inch deck will not fit through a standard 48-inch gate and may crowd a single garage bay, and it costs well into the prosumer range. If your property is closer to one or two acres, the smaller picks below will do the job for much less."
  },
  {
    "id": "ariens-ikon-xd-52",
    "name": "Ariens IKON XD 52-Inch",
    "category": "Fabricated 52-in. Deck | Mid-Price",
    "badge": "Best Value",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Fabricated deck at a price near stamped-deck rivals",
      "52-inch width fits more gates and trailers than 60-inch machines",
      "Simple, robust frame with good owner reputation",
      "Easy-access deck height adjustment",
      "Strong residential warranty coverage"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Entry-level transaxles compared with prosumer models",
      "Ride is firmer than suspension-seat machines"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The most durable deck you can get without stepping into prosumer pricing.",
    "description": "The IKON XD is the value story in zero-turns because it pairs a fabricated deck with a price that sits close to many stamped-deck mowers. A fabricated deck is the part that tends to survive stumps, roots and years of debris without bending or rusting through, so getting one at this level is a meaningful advantage. At 52 inches, the width is a sweet spot for one to three acres: big enough to finish quickly, narrow enough to fit through many gates and onto a standard utility trailer. Owners commonly praise the simple frame and how easy it is to service the deck. The trade-off is the drivetrain. The transaxles are residential units, fine for a typical homeowner duty cycle but not designed for commercial hours. Ride comfort is also basic, so long sessions on rough ground are more tiring than on machines with suspension. For most buyers mowing a couple of acres, that is an acceptable compromise for the price."
  },
  {
    "id": "husqvarna-z254f",
    "name": "Husqvarna Z254F",
    "category": "Fabricated 54-in. Deck | Kawasaki Power",
    "badge": "Best for 1-2 Acres",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "54-inch fabricated deck with good cut quality",
      "Kawasaki V-twin engine with a solid reputation",
      "Comfortable high-back seat and easy controls",
      "Widely sold through dealers and big-box stores",
      "Reasonable price for its build"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Residential transaxles limit heavy-duty use",
      "Some owners report the seat is basic for very long sessions"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A balanced, well-built zero-turn for a one-to-two-acre property at a fair price.",
    "description": "Husqvarna's Z254F combines a 54-inch fabricated deck with a Kawasaki V-twin, a pairing that shows up in many owner recommendations for mid-size properties. The engine choice matters: Kawasaki's residential twins have a good reputation for starting reliably and running smoothly over years of use, and parts are easy to source. The fabricated deck gives the same durability advantage as the Ariens. For one to two acres of mostly level grass with trees and beds, 54 inches is a comfortable width that does not feel unwieldy around obstacles. Husqvarna sells through both dealers and large retailers, which helps with availability and service. As with most mid-priced zero-turns, the transaxles are residential grade, so treat it as a homeowner machine and do not expect commercial hours from it. If your yard has noticeable slopes, read the slope section below before buying any zero-turn, this one included."
  },
  {
    "id": "john-deere-z530m",
    "name": "John Deere Z530M",
    "category": "Deere Dealer Network | Residential",
    "badge": "Best Dealer Support",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "John Deere dealer network is the widest for service",
      "Comfortable seat and refined controls",
      "Strong resale value",
      "Good cut quality from the Accel Deep deck",
      "Easy-change oil and service points"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Costs more than similar-spec competitors",
      "Stamped deck on many trims"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy it when a nearby Deere dealer and future resale matter as much as the spec sheet.",
    "description": "The Z530M is a residential zero-turn whose biggest selling point is the badge and what comes with it. John Deere's dealer network is the broadest of any mower brand in much of the country, and that matters when something needs service in the middle of mowing season. Deere machines also hold resale value well, which takes some of the sting out of a higher purchase price. The mower itself is comfortable, with a supportive seat, smooth lap-bar controls and a deck that owners commonly say leaves a clean, even cut. The weakness compared with the Ariens and Husqvarna is that you pay more for similar mechanical specifications, and many configurations use a stamped deck rather than a fabricated one. For a buyer who values a local dealer relationship and expects to trade the mower in someday, that premium is easy to justify. For one who does their own maintenance and keeps machines until they wear out, the cheaper picks give more hardware per dollar."
  },
  {
    "id": "cub-cadet-ultima-zt1",
    "name": "Cub Cadet Ultima ZT1 50-Inch",
    "category": "Stamped 50-in. Deck | Entry Price",
    "badge": "Best Budget Pick",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Low entry price for a full-size zero-turn",
      "50-inch deck suits one to two acres",
      "Steel frame and AeroForce-style deck for decent cut",
      "Widely available at big-box stores",
      "Comfortable enough for weekly mowing"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Entry-level hydros wear faster under hard use",
      "Stamped deck is more prone to damage than fabricated"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A sensible first zero-turn for a flat one-to-two-acre lawn if budget is the main limit.",
    "description": "The Ultima ZT1 is a common first zero-turn because it brings the speed and maneuverability of the format down to a price close to a mid-range lawn tractor. A 50-inch stamped deck is a reasonable size for one to two acres, and the steel frame is sturdier than the very cheapest zero-turns. Owners commonly report a good cut on flat lawns and appreciate how easy it is to buy and return through big-box stores. The compromises are where you would expect. The transaxles are entry-level units that are adequate for a homeowner's weekly mow but wear faster if you push hard on slopes or tow, and the stamped deck can bend if you hit stumps or rocks. Keep the deck clean, keep speeds sensible on rough ground and change the hydro fluid if the manual allows, and it should give a typical homeowner years of service. If you can stretch the budget, a fabricated deck is the most worthwhile upgrade."
  },
  {
    "id": "ego-z6-electric",
    "name": "EGO Power+ Z6 42-Inch",
    "category": "56V Battery | 42-in. Deck | Lap Bar",
    "badge": "Best Electric",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "No gas, oil changes or engine maintenance",
      "Very quiet compared with gas zero-turns",
      "Battery bays use EGO's 56V packs",
      "Instant start and simple controls",
      "42-inch deck fits through narrower gates"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Battery packs are expensive to add or replace",
      "Runtime limits it to smaller acreage than gas models"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The battery zero-turn for one to two acres when quiet and low maintenance matter more than all-day runtime.",
    "description": "The EGO Z6 is one of the best-known battery zero-turns, and it makes the case for electric riding well. It runs on EGO's 56-volt batteries loaded into bays under the seat, so there is no gas, no oil, no spark plugs and no winterizing. It is dramatically quieter than a gas zero-turn, which owners frequently mention as the reason they bought it. The 42-inch deck is smaller than the gas picks here, which helps it fit through gates and maneuver in tighter yards, and it is comfortable for one to roughly two acres depending on the battery configuration. The trade-offs are runtime and cost. More acreage means more batteries, and each high-capacity pack adds a significant amount to the price. Heavy, wet grass also shortens runtime. For a buyer with a mid-size lawn who wants to stop dealing with small engines, it is a compelling choice. For anyone mowing three acres or more each week, gas remains the practical answer."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Zero-Turn Mower Picks",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-zero-turn-mower/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best zero-turn mower for most homeowners?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "On most one-to-three-acre properties, the Ariens IKON XD 52 or Husqvarna Z254F offers the best blend of fabricated-deck durability and fair pricing. If you cut three acres or more and want a mower that lasts well over a decade, the Toro TITAN MAX is the better long-term buy. How close the nearest dealer is should weigh heavily in the final choice."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size zero-turn do I need for two acres?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A deck of about 50 to 54 inches suits most two-acre properties. It covers ground quickly without feeling clumsy around trees and beds. On a mostly open lot with long runs, 60 inches can save time, but measure your gates, shed door and trailer first, because a 60-inch deck will not fit through many standard openings."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many acres can a residential zero-turn mow?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most residential zero-turns are sized for one to five acres, depending on deck width and build quality. Entry models suit one to two acres, upper residential models handle two to four, and prosumer machines are built for larger properties and heavier weekly use. Battery models generally do best on about one to two acres."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you use a zero-turn mower on a hill?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only on gentle slopes, and only within the limit in the operator manual, often about 10 to 15 degrees for residential models. Zero-turns steer through the rear wheels, so they can lose control on wet grass or steep ground. Take slopes slowly, drive straight up and down them, and use a walk-behind or trimmer on steep banks."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a zero-turn mower last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "With good care, entry-level residential zero-turns often last several years to roughly a decade, and upper residential or prosumer models with fabricated decks and better transaxles commonly last much longer. The biggest factors are the transaxles, how much acreage you cut, whether you mow slopes, and how well you keep up with blades, oil and deck cleaning."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a fabricated deck worth the extra money?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually, if you mow around trees, roots, rocks or rough edges. Fabricated decks are welded from thicker steel and resist dents and rust far better than stamped decks. On a smooth, obstacle-free lawn a well-kept stamped deck can last a long time, so the upgrade matters less. Ask the dealer which deck the exact trim you are quoted has."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I buy a zero-turn or a lawn tractor?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Pick a zero-turn for mostly level properties over about an acre with lots of trees and beds, where speed and maneuverability save real time. Pick a lawn tractor for hilly ground, towing, snow removal or ground-engaging attachments. Tractors steer with the front wheels and are generally more stable on slopes."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Lawn Mowers & Tractors",
    tags: [
      "best zero turn mower",
      "zero-turn mower",
      "residential zero turn",
      "fabricated deck mower",
      "zero turn transaxle",
      "mowing large yards"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <header data-hero class="bg-[#F4F6F8] border-b border-gray-200">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-10 pb-8 lg:pt-14 lg:pb-10">
        <p class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest m-0 mb-4">
          <span class="text-[#D93A00]">Buyer's Guide</span>
          <span class="text-gray-300" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
          <span class="text-gray-500">Updated {dates.display}</span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="text-[2.1rem] leading-[1.15] sm:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight text-gray-900 m-0">
          Best Zero Turn Mower
        </h1>
        <QuickAnswer products={PRODUCTS} />
        <p class="text-lg lg:text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-5 mb-0">
          The Toro TITAN MAX 60-inch is the best zero turn mower for three acres or more, while the Ariens IKON XD 52 and Husqvarna Z254F suit most one-to-three-acre lots for less money. Slopes, gate widths and the transaxles change that answer more than horsepower does. This guide covers all six picks, deck sizing, slope limits, price tiers and upkeep.
        </p>
        <!-- Author & Meta -->
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mt-7">
          <AuthorAvatar size={40} />
          <div class="min-w-0">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 m-0">Written by <a href="/authors/alex-rivers/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Alex Rivers</a></p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 m-0">Home Improvement Editor &middot; Updated {dates.display}</p>
          </div>
          <SocialProof users="" />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-6">
          <TrustBadge />
          <span class="flex-1 h-px bg-gray-300"></span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-3 mb-0">
          Based on manufacturer specs for transaxles, decks, engines and warranty, dealer reach and owner feedback. Not hands-on tested. No paid placements. <a href="/how-we-test/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 underline">Read how we pick</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <details open class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl px-5 pt-4 pb-2">
        <summary class="flex items-center gap-3 pb-3 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" d="M9 6h11M9 12h11M9 18h11M4.5 6h.01M4.5 12h.01M4.5 18h.01" /></svg>
          <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">In This Guide</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-semibold text-gray-400">16 sections</span>
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 ml-auto text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M6 15l6-6 6 6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6">
              <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">1.</span><span>The Short Answer</span></a>
              <a href="#top-picks" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">2.</span><span>The 6 Best Zero-Turns</span></a>
              <a href="#is-it-right" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">3.</span><span>Is a Zero-Turn Worth It?</span></a>
              <a href="#slopes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">4.</span><span>Can It Handle Hills?</span></a>
              <a href="#deck-width" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">5.</span><span>What Deck Size You Need</span></a>
              <a href="#transaxles" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">6.</span><span>Why Transaxles Matter</span></a>
              <a href="#decks" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">7.</span><span>Fabricated vs Stamped Decks</span></a>
              <a href="#engines" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">8.</span><span>Horsepower and Electric</span></a>
              <a href="#comfort" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">9.</span><span>Comfort and Controls</span></a>
              <a href="#cut-finish" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">10.</span><span>Mulching and Bagging</span></a>
              <a href="#where-to-buy" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>Dealer or Big-Box Store?</span></a>
              <a href="#maintenance" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">12.</span><span>Service Intervals</span></a>
              <a href="#storage" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">13.</span><span>Storage and Trailering</span></a>
              <a href="#price-tiers" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">14.</span><span>What a Zero-Turn Costs</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">15.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">16.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Think of a zero-turn as a machine built for speed. It pays for itself on big, mostly flat lawns crowded with trees and beds, where it pivots around obstacles at a pace no lawn tractor can keep. It is the wrong buy for steep ground, for towing, and for anyone who sizes the deck from acreage alone. Answer the terrain question first; after that, the choice comes down to how many years you want the mower to last.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- Short Answer -->
        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Best Zero Turn Mower: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            For three acres or more of open turf, the <strong>Toro TITAN MAX 60-Inch</strong> is the long-haul pick, with a fabricated deck and commercial-grade transaxles. Most one-to-three-acre owners get the best value from the <strong>Ariens IKON XD 52</strong> or the <strong>Husqvarna Z254F</strong>. Choose the <strong>John Deere Z530M</strong> when a nearby dealer matters most, the <strong>Cub Cadet Ultima ZT1</strong> when budget rules on a flat lot, and the <strong>EGO Z6</strong> when you want to quit gas on a smaller lawn.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Mower</th>
