        <div id="is-it-right" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is a Zero-Turn Mower Worth It for Your Property?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It is worth it once you have more than about an acre of mostly level grass with plenty of trees, beds and fence lines to work around. A zero-turn steers by spinning its two rear wheels at different speeds through a pair of hydrostatic transaxles. Push one lap bar forward and pull the other back and the mower turns on the spot. That makes it much quicker around obstacles than a lawn tractor, which has to swing wide arcs and make three-point turns, and on a busy property it can shrink total mowing time dramatically.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What can't a zero-turn do?</h3>

          <p>
            The speed pitch leaves out the limits. With no steering wheel and steering only through the rear wheels, a zero-turn loses control more easily on slopes and wet grass. Most are not built to tow heavy loads or run ground-engaging attachments. If you need to haul a dump cart, pull an aerator over a sloped yard or plow snow, a lawn tractor or a <a href="/best-compact-tractor/">compact tractor</a> is the better machine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What if you have less than an acre?</h3>

          <p>
            Under about an acre, a good lawn tractor or even a wide walk-behind is usually cheaper and simpler. If you sit right on the line, our <a href="/best-riding-lawn-mower/">riding mower guide</a> covers both lawn tractors and small zero-turns.
          </p>

          <p>
            How much you value your time is the other test. Someone who enjoys a long, unhurried mow on a tractor gains little from a zero-turn. Someone who wants a three-acre lawn finished in an hour instead of two will notice the difference every weekend for as long as they own the machine.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Rule of thumb</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">More than an acre, mostly flat, full of obstacles: zero-turn. Hills, towing or snow: tractor.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A zero-turn does one job, mowing, very well. When the property needs a machine that does more than mow, a tractor's versatility usually wins.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="slopes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Can a Zero-Turn Mower Handle Hills and Slopes?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Only gentle ones, and only within the limit printed in the operator manual, which for residential zero-turns commonly falls around 10 to 15 degrees. Slopes are the most common reason a zero-turn proves to be the wrong purchase. Because steering and braking both run through the rear drive wheels, losing traction on a slope means losing both at once. On wet grass or loose soil the mower can slide sideways or downhill, and the front casters do nothing to stop it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you check your own slopes?</h3>

          <p>
            Look up the maximum recommended slope for the exact model you are considering and treat it cautiously, since that figure assumes dry grass, good tires and careful technique. Many owners use an inclinometer app on a smartphone to measure their steepest spots before they buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How should you mow a slope safely?</h3>

          <p>
            Most zero-turn manuals tell you to drive straight up and down a slope rather than across it, and to avoid sharp turns on any incline. Keep the rollover protection structure raised and wear the seat belt whenever a ROPS is fitted. Stay well away from drop-offs, ditches, retaining walls and pond banks where a wheel could go over the edge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where part of the property is steep, mow that section with a walk-behind or cut it with a string trimmer. A rear-drive <a href="/best-self-propelled-lawn-mower/">self-propelled mower</a> handles banks no rider should try, and plenty of large-property owners keep one beside the zero-turn for exactly that reason. A few extra minutes on the hill beats putting a heavy machine on ground it was never designed for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-width" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Size Zero-Turn Do You Need for Your Acreage?
          </h2>

          <p>
            As a starting point, 42 to 48 inches suits about one acre, 50 to 54 inches suits one to three acres, and 60 inches or more suits three acres and up of open turf. Size down for obstacles and narrow gates, and up for long, open runs. Deck width decides how much grass each pass cuts, but acreage is only part of the answer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Will the mower fit through your gate?</h3>

          <p>
            Measure every gate and opening the mower has to pass through, and the garage or shed door where it will be parked. Counting the discharge chute, a 60-inch deck is roughly five and a half feet wide, so it will not go through a standard 48-inch garden gate at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is a wider deck always faster?</h3>

          <p>
            Not on a crowded lot. A wide deck saves time on open lawn but slows you down in yards full of trees, beds and narrow strips, because you trim more with the deck's edge and make extra passes around obstacles. On a busy one-to-two-acre property, a 48- or 52-inch machine is often faster overall than a 60-inch on the same ground.
          </p>

          <p>
            Anyone who hauls the mower between properties should measure the trailer too. Check the inside width between the fenders before settling on a 60-inch deck.
          </p>

