              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Per manual</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Hydro fluid service</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. How Do You Store and Trailer a Zero-Turn Mower?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Give it more floor space than you expect, back it up the ramp, strap the frame, and prepare the fuel and battery before winter. Beyond the deck width, measure the overall length including the foot platform and any rear bagger, then make sure you can still open a car door or reach the workbench with the mower parked. Some owners put up a simple lean-to or buy a shed just for the mower, and that space belongs in the budget.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you load a zero-turn on a trailer?</h3>

          <p>
            Use a ramp rated for the mower plus the operator's weight, and back the mower up it, which keeps the heavier rear end low and lowers the risk of tipping. Strap the frame rather than the deck or lap bars, and set the parking brake. Many owners tie down at four points: the rear frame and the front caster forks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you winterize a gas zero-turn?</h3>

          <p>
            Either run the engine dry or fill the tank with fresh, stabilized fuel and run it for a few minutes so the treated fuel reaches the carburetor. Change the oil while it is warm, clean the deck, sharpen the blades and charge or remove the battery. A maintainer keeps a lead-acid starting battery healthy through the cold months.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How should battery packs be stored?</h3>

          <p>
            Indoors, somewhere cool and dry, at a moderate charge of roughly half full, as most manufacturers advise. Do not leave lithium packs in a freezing shed for months. In spring, check tire pressure, look over belts and hoses, and grease any fittings before the first mow.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-tiers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            14. How Much Does a Good Zero-Turn Mower Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Roughly $3,000 to $4,500 for entry residential, $4,500 to $6,500 for upper residential and $6,500 to $10,000 or more for prosumer machines at the time of writing. Each step up buys a tougher deck, a better drivetrain or both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does each tier get you?</h3>

          <p>
            Entry residential mowers typically come with stamped decks, entry-level sealed transaxles and basic seats. They are fine for flat lawns of one to two acres cut weekly by a careful owner, and they should give several years of good service if you keep them off slopes and away from heavy work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Upper residential models add fabricated decks, better engines, more comfortable seats and sometimes serviceable transaxles. For most buyers with two to four acres, this tier offers the best balance of price and lifespan.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prosumer machines borrow from commercial lines with heavy fabricated decks, commercial-grade transaxles, suspension and wider deck options. They make sense on large properties, for owners who plan to keep a mower well beyond a decade, and for anyone who mows several properties.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How should you price a battery model?</h3>

          <p>
            Battery zero-turns cover a wide price range depending on how many packs they carry. Price the setup your acreage actually requires, extra packs included, before comparing it with gas. At any tier, budget for a trailer, spare blades and dealer service as well.
          </p>

