        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            15. Common Mistakes With Zero-Turn Mowers
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a zero-turn on a hilly property</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A zero-turn steers and brakes through its rear wheels alone, so on slopes and wet grass it can lose grip and slide. Look up the manual's maximum slope, commonly about 10 to 15 degrees, measure your steepest ground, and hand anything steeper to a walk-behind or tractor. No mowing schedule justifies a rollover.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing the deck from acreage alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 60-inch deck sounds efficient on four acres, yet it may not clear your gate, fit your trailer or get around tight obstacles. Measure gates, shed doors and trailer width first, then judge how much of the yard is truly open turf. On a crowded lot, a narrower deck often finishes sooner.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overlooking the transaxles</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Transaxles set a zero-turn's lifespan. Entry-level sealed units suit light homeowner use but wear fast under hard work. With big acreage or slopes, pay for serviceable or commercial-grade transaxles, and ask the dealer for the transaxle model number and whether its fluid can be changed before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting blades and the deck slide</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dull blades and a dirty, out-of-level deck lead to a poor cut, scalping and extra strain on the engine. Sharpen every 20 to 25 hours, scrape the deck after wet mows and check its level each season. Most complaints about cut quality come back to these three basics.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the mower like a tow vehicle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most residential zero-turns are not designed to pull heavy carts or ground-engaging attachments. Towing strains the transaxles and can turn dangerous on a slope. If the property needs hauling or attachments, a lawn tractor or compact tractor is the proper tool; light carts on level ground are usually the limit.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

