        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            16. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best zero-turn mower for most homeowners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On most one-to-three-acre properties, the Ariens IKON XD 52 or Husqvarna Z254F offers the best blend of fabricated-deck durability and fair pricing. If you cut three acres or more and want a mower that lasts well over a decade, the Toro TITAN MAX is the better long-term buy. How close the nearest dealer is should weigh heavily in the final choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size zero-turn do I need for two acres?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A deck of about 50 to 54 inches suits most two-acre properties. It covers ground quickly without feeling clumsy around trees and beds. On a mostly open lot with long runs, 60 inches can save time, but measure your gates, shed door and trailer first, because a 60-inch deck will not fit through many standard openings.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many acres can a residential zero-turn mow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most residential zero-turns are sized for one to five acres, depending on deck width and build quality. Entry models suit one to two acres, upper residential models handle two to four, and prosumer machines are built for larger properties and heavier weekly use. Battery models generally do best on about one to two acres.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a zero-turn mower on a hill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only on gentle slopes, and only within the limit in the operator manual, often about 10 to 15 degrees for residential models. Zero-turns steer through the rear wheels, so they can lose control on wet grass or steep ground. Take slopes slowly, drive straight up and down them, and use a walk-behind or trimmer on steep banks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a zero-turn mower last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With good care, entry-level residential zero-turns often last several years to roughly a decade, and upper residential or prosumer models with fabricated decks and better transaxles commonly last much longer. The biggest factors are the transaxles, how much acreage you cut, whether you mow slopes, and how well you keep up with blades, oil and deck cleaning.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a fabricated deck worth the extra money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, if you mow around trees, roots, rocks or rough edges. Fabricated decks are welded from thicker steel and resist dents and rust far better than stamped decks. On a smooth, obstacle-free lawn a well-kept stamped deck can last a long time, so the upgrade matters less. Ask the dealer which deck the exact trim you are quoted has.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a zero-turn or a lawn tractor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pick a zero-turn for mostly level properties over about an acre with lots of trees and beds, where speed and maneuverability save real time. Pick a lawn tractor for hilly ground, towing, snow removal or ground-engaging attachments. Tractors steer with the front wheels and are generally more stable on slopes.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

